% Draft 2 of Lesson 17's lending policy, with the policy stated a second
% time: as integrity constraints, the things that must never happen.
% The rules are still wrong, and now the engine says so unprompted.
%
%   python3 datalog.py programs/lending-draft2-checked.dl

member(iris).      member(jon).      member(kim).      member(lena).
staff(kim).        staff(lena).

% loans recorded as overdue, by whoever runs circulation
overdue(jon, book_a).
overdue(lena, book_b).     % staff may borrow despite overdue loans

% suspensions are a decision made elsewhere, not derived here
suspended(kim).

% Draft 3.  Every negation points at a base fact, so this stratifies
% and has exactly one model — see the lesson for why drafts 1 and 2
% did not survive review.
has_overdue(P) :- overdue(P, _).

may_borrow(P) :- member(P), not has_overdue(P), not suspended(P).
may_borrow(P) :- staff(P).

% The policy in English, one constraint per clause of it.  A constraint
% has no head: its body is a situation, and every way it can be matched
% is a counterexample.
:- may_borrow(P), suspended(P).                  % suspension applies to everybody
:- may_borrow(P), has_overdue(P), not staff(P).  % overdue members wait
:- may_borrow(P), not member(P).                 % only members borrow
